# Code quality

## Architecture

- Prefer small, composable modules with clear boundaries over monolithic files.
- Keep business logic out of UI and transport layers; isolate side effects at the edges.
- Do not introduce new frameworks, ORMs, or infra services without an explicit request.
- Match the project's existing stack, naming, and folder conventions before inventing new ones.
- Prefer explicit configuration and typed interfaces over implicit globals.
- Prefer extend over rewrite unless the current design is clearly wrong.
- Keep public APIs stable; if you must break them, call out migration steps.
- Leave the tree cleaner than you found it, but avoid drive-by refactors outside the task.

## General craft

- SOLID, DRY, KISS, YAGNI. One responsibility per module; never duplicate logic; simplest
  readable solution; do not build what is not needed yet.
- Self-documenting code: names MUST state exact purpose. No vague names or intent-obscuring
  abbreviations.
- Comments explain WHY (business rule, edge case, trade-off), NEVER WHAT. Never narrate the
  edit being made. Use `TODO:` for documented future work.
- Write code for the next reader: clear names, small functions, obvious control flow.
- Handle errors deliberately; never swallow exceptions without logging or rethrowing.
- Avoid premature abstraction; duplicate twice before extracting once.
- Prefer pure helpers for reusable logic; keep I/O at the boundary.

## Language defaults (when the project uses them)

### TypeScript / JavaScript

- Prefer `strict` typing; avoid `any` unless temporarily bridging untyped code (and note why).
- Use `async`/`await` over raw promise chains; propagate failures with useful context.
- Colocate types with usage; export only what other modules need.

### Python

- Prefer type hints on public functions and return values.
- Use context managers for resources; fail fast on invalid input.
- Follow the project's formatter/linter; do not mix styles in one change.

## Tests

- Add or update tests when behavior changes and the project already has a test suite.
- Prefer focused unit/integration coverage over brittle end-to-end snapshots for pure logic.
- Worktree proof / idle-main complete live in the `testing` rule.
